    ├── Pitfalls   : ipaddress uses ValueError/AddressValueError instead of AddrFormatError — exception handling must be updated; 
    │   ipaddress IPv4Network/IPv6Network are separate classes, not a single IPNetwork class — type checks need adjustment; EUI (MAC 
    │   address) parsing is not in stdlib ipaddress; would need netaddr retained or a separate MAC library; ipaddress is stricter on 
    │   input formats (e.g., no implicit netmask inference like netaddr's '192.168.1.1/24' vs '192.168.1.1'); netaddr's 
    │   IPNetwork(value) preserves host bits by default; ipaddress.IPv4Network(value, strict=False) required for same behavior
    └── The code uses netaddr's IPAddress, IPNetwork, EUI, and AddrFormatError for parsing, validating, and manipulating IP addresses, 
        CIDR networks, and MAC addresses. Python's standard library ipaddress module (available since Python 3.3) provides IPAddress 
        (as ipaddress.IPv4Address/IPv6Address), IPNetwork (as ipaddress.IPv4Network/IPv6Network), and equivalent exception handling 
        (ValueError/AddressValueError). EUI (MAC address) support would require netaddr or a separate library like macaddress, but the 
        core IP functionality is covered by ipaddress.

