    │   Qt.AlignmentFlag.AlignLeft); PyQt uses pyqtSignal/pyqtSlot; PySide uses Signal/Slot — signal/slot syntax differs; PyQt5/6 are 
    │   GPL/commercial; PySide2/6 are LGPL — licensing implications for distribution; exec_() vs exec() method naming differs between 
    │   Qt5 and Qt6 bindings; PySide6 requires Python 3.6+; PyQt5 supports older Python versions
    └── The code uses PyQt5.QtWidgets for building a desktop GUI form with standard widgets (labels, line edits, checkboxes, grid 
        layout). PySide6, PySide2, and PyQt6 are all Qt bindings that provide the same QtWidgets module with nearly identical APIs. 
        PySide6 is the current official Qt for Python binding (LGPL), PySide2 is the older Qt5 version, and PyQt6 is the Riverbank 
        Computing binding for Qt6. All four can serve as drop-in replacements for the widget classes used here with only import path 
        changes.

